{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_amxlfl4hmzxxt2smq2q5crxhfrd2jjbwppoa6d3u4y6w4xonjyza","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All tests pass in both debug (3.04s) and release modes, well within the 60-second timeout.\n\n## Summary\n\nI translated the GFC (Generalized Feistel Cipher) C library to safe, idiomatic Rust by implementing all `unimplemented!()` bodies in `src/gfc.rs`:\n\n- **`ror`/`rol`**: Used Rust's built-in `rotate_right`/`rotate_left` u32 methods.\n- **`round`**: Implemented the Speck round function using `wrapping_add` for the modular arithmetic.\n- **`speck_expand`**: Speck key schedule, generating `SPECK_ROUNDS` round keys from a 4-word key.\n- **`speck_encrypt`**: Speck block cipher encryption (27 rounds operating on a 64-bit block as two u32 halves).\n- **`GFC::new`**: Constructs the GFC with `a = b = ceil(sqrt(range))`, then expands `r` independent Speck round-key schedules. The C code reinterprets a `uint64_t key[2] = {seed, i}` as `uint32_t[4]`, which I emulated explicitly with little-endian splits.\n- **`GFC::f`**: Random function `F_j(R)` selecting the right round-key block and packing/unpacking 64-bit values as little-endian u32 pairs to match the C code's pointer cast.\n- **`GFC::fe`/`GFC::fe_inv`**: The Feistel forward and inverse functions, including the underflow-safe subtraction `(R + a - F % a) % a` from the C code.\n- **`GFC::encrypt`/`GFC::decrypt`**: Cycle-walking around `fe`/`fe_inv` until the result lies in `[0, M)`.\n\nThe implementation is fully safe Rust (no `unsafe`, no FFI), doesn't modify struct definitions, signatures, `lib.rs`, or `bin/`, and passes `cargo test` in both debug and release modes."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":419,"cache_read_input_tokens":26862,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":419,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_t